      "abstract": "ABSTRACT The most inadequately treated dimension in recent American geographical writing is the \u201cvertical'\u2019dimension of ecological relationships. Man's impact on the land has been widely recognized, but influences from the natural environment upon man have been ignored. In Russia, however, despite the Marxist principle that economic causation is primary in social development, V. A. Anuchin has recently emphasized what I term \u201cenvironmental causation.'\u2019He has found \u201cgeographical determinism,'\u2019or an overestimation of environmental causation, to be less dangerous than indeterminism, or the denial of causal relationships. Such views have led Anuchin to claim, contrary to Soviet tradition, that land should be given a price. Common basis exists for Soviet and Western geographers to combine an appreciation of environmental causation with forward-looking \u201cidealistic'\u2019approaches to remaking the landscape. Key Words: V. A. AnuchinEcological dimensianEnvironmental causationIdealismIndeterminismMarxist geographical theoryMan-land relationshipsEdward J. Taaffe Notes \u2217 Part of this study was carried out while I held a research fellowship at the Russian Research Center, Harvard University, during 1973\u201374. The Geography Department at the University of Washington provided a favorable setting for my initial study of Soviet geographic theory in 1962. My interpretations of Marxist-Leninist philosophy, geographical and otherwise, owe a great deal to my study of Russian Intellectual History under Martin Malia, at the University of California at Berkeley during 1960\u201361. I also wish to thank Richard Hartshorne, whose advice has helped me think more clearly about the terminology involved in man-land theory. 1 Carl O. Sauer, \u201cThe Fourth Dimension of Geography,\u201dAnnals, Association of American Geographers, Vol. 64 (1974), pp. 189 2; and Richard Hartshorne, \u201cThe Nature of Geography,\u201dAnnals, Association of American Geographers, Vol. 29 (1939), pp. 351 64. 2 Edward J. Taaffe, \u201cThe Spatial View in Context,\u201dAnnals, Association of American Geographers, Vol. 64 (1974), pp. 1 16. 3 The common ground between the regional-study and the spatial-analysis approaches to geography has recently been explored in detail by Robert D. Sack, \u201cChorology and Spatial Analysis,\u201dAnnals, Association of American Geographers, Vol. 64 (1974), pp. 439 52. 4 Taaffe, op. cit., footnote 2, p. 1. 5 A leading ethologist's view of the relation between environmental and genetic factors is Konrad Lorenz, Evolution and Modification of Behavior. Stephen Jay Gould, \u201cThe Nonscience of Human Nature,\u201dNatural History, Vol. 83, No. 4 (April, 1974), pp. 21\u201324, laments the deterioration of such views into ill-supported claims of innate aggressiveness: \u201cWith Konrad Lorenz as godfather, Robert Ardrey as dramatist, and Desmond Morris as raconteur, we are presented with the behavior of man, \u2018the naked ape,\u2019 descended from an African carnivore, innately aggressive and inherently territorial\u201d (p. 21). Elsewhere, Gould reflects the conclusions of Ernst Mayr, Richard Lewontin, and other biologists that \u201cthe expression of any trait represents a complex interaction of heredity and environment,\u201d and thus that Arthur Jensen cannot rightly claim that I.Q. is eighty per cent heritable: Stephen Jay Gould, \u201cRacist Arguments and I.Q.,\u201dNatural History, Vol. 73, No. 5 (May, 1974), pp. 24\u201329; quotation from p. 29. More technical presentations of the same case are found in Richard C. Lewontin, \u201cThe Analysis of Variance and the Analysis of Causes,\u201dAmerican Journal of Human Genetics, Vol. 26 (1974), pp. 400 11; and in Alexander Alland Jr., Human Diversity (Garden City, N. Y.: Doubleday Anchor Books, 1973), especially pp. 172\u2013208. 6 Taaffe, op. cit., footnote 2, pp. 3 and 5. 7 Ellsworth Huntington, The Human Habitat (New York: Van Nostrand, 1927), p. 19. 8 This is illustrated, for example, in the work of Clark Akatiff. His \u201cThe March on the Pentagon,\u201dAnnals, Association of American Geographers, Vol. 64 (1974), pp. 26\u201333, analyzed antiwar political dissidence in terms of spatial patterns and environmental perception; environmental causation theory was not appropriate to that purpose, but it has entered prominently into other parts of Akatiff's writing. His \u201cAn Evaluation of Some Geographical Concepts of Ellsworth Huntington,\u201d unpublished master's thesis, University of California at Los Angeles, 1963, was the first long study of Huntington's environmental determinism by a geographer of the younger generation. 9 Carl O. Sauer, \u201cThe Education of a Geographer,\u201dAnnals, Association of American Geographers, Vol. 46 (1956), pp. 287 99; reprinted in John Leighly, ed., Land and Life (Berkeley and Los Angeles: University of California Press, 1963), pp. 389\u2013404. For an alternative to the Sauer-Leighly skepticism on causation, with references to his earlier writings, see John E. Chappell, Jr., \u201cOn Causation in Geographical Theory,\u201dProceedings, Association of American Geographers, Vol. 1 (1969), pp. 34\u201338; and John E. Chappell, Jr., \u201cThe Incomplete Chain of Causation: Barrier to Problem-Solving,\u201dJournal of Geography, Vol. 71 (1972), pp. 452 56, especially footnote 10 on p. 454. 10 O. H. K. Spate, \u201cEllsworth Huntington: a Geographical Giant\u201d (review of Geoffrey J. Martin, Ellsworth Huntington: His Life and Thought), Geographical Journal, Vol. 140 (1974), pp. 117\u201319. See also O. H. K. Spate, \u201cToynbee and Huntington: a Study in Determinism,\u201d Geographical Journal, Vol. 118 (1952); reprinted in O. H. K. Spate, Let Me Enjoy: Essays, Partly Geographical (London: Methuen, 1966), pp. 185\u2013211. 11 Jean Brunhes, Human Geography, abridged edition, ed. by Mme. M. Jean-Brunhes Delamarre and Pierre Deffontaines (London: George Harrap and Co., 1952), p. 223. On the same page Brunhes wrote \u201cthe geographical setting remains the same, but the men who dwell in it have wants that are constantly growing, changing, and becoming more complex.\u201d This statement represents a dominant concept in Western geography over recent decades, but Brunhes to some extent contradicted it in the same volume, in a section titled \u201cThe Principle of Activity:\u201d\u201cEverything around us is in a state of change \u2026 nothing is really stable. The level of the sea \u2026 The vast icefields \u2026 The loftiest peaks \u2026 Changes [in these] proceed so gradually that they are scarcely perceptible by a single generation of men, but it would be a grave mistake to ignore them\u201d (p. 20). 12 Lucien Febvre, A Geographical Introduction to History (New York: Barnes and Noble, 1966; first published 1924), p. 114, admitted that he had not been able to examine his copy of Huntington's Civilization and Climate (1915). 13 To compare Huntington's views to Semple's is to miss some crucial distinctions; such an error was made in the comment following John E. Chappell, Jr., \u201cHarlan Barrows and Environmentalism,\u201dAnnals, Association of American Geographers, Vol. 61 (1971), pp. 198 201. Semple did not even accept Huntington's ideas on climatic change. 14 Taaffe, op. cit., footnote 2, p. 16. Less essential problems with Taaffe's presentation include his use of the term \u201cholistic\u201d to imply a comprehensive, almanac-style approach to geography (p. 6). The dictionary definition of the term, and the one generally used by philosophers and historians, is \u201cemphasizing the organic or functional relation between parts and wholes,\u201d with \u201catomistic\u201d given as an approximate antonym. Thus the \u201cpaucity of cumulative generalizations,\u201d which Taaffe bemoaned as prevailing during the heyday of regional studies in the 1930s, does not indicate a holistic approach, but instead something more akin to a breakdown into an atomistic cataloguing\u2014i.e., almost the opposite. 15 V. A. Anuchin, Teoreticheskie Osnovy Geografii (Theoretical Foundations of Geography) (Moskva: \u201cMysl',\u201d 1972). 16 V. A. Anuchin, Teoreticheskie Problemy Geografii (Theoretical Problems of Geography) (Moskva: Gosud. Izdatel'stvo Geograf. Lit., 1960). An English translation of this book by Steven Shabad, edited by David J. M. Hooson, exists and may soon be published. A number of Anuchin's key articles have appeared in English translations in Soviet Geography: Review and Translation throughout the 1960s and early 1970s. For Western views of the controversy surrounding Anuchin, and of related trends in Soviet geography, see David J. M. Hooson, \u201cMethodological Clashes in Moscow,\u201dAnnals, Association of American Geographers, Vol. 52 (1962), pp. 469 75; O. H. K. Spate, \u201cTheory and Practice in Soviet Geography,\u201dAustralian Geographical Studies, Vol. 1 (1963), pp. 18 30; John E. Chappell, Jr., \u201cMarxism and Geography,\u201dProblems of Communism, Vol. 14, No. 6 (Nov.-Dec. 1965), pp. 12\u201322; George Demko, \u201cTrends in Soviet Geography,\u201dSurvey, No. 55 (April, 1965), pp. 163\u201370; Ian Matley, \u201cThe Marxist Approach to the Geographical Environment,\u201dAnnals, Association of American Geographers, Vol. 56 (1966), pp. 97 111; and John E. Chappell, Jr., \u201cMarxism and Environmentalism,\u201dAnnals, Association of American Geographers, Vol. 56 (1967), pp. 203 06, with rejoinder by Matley. For a summary by one of Anuchin's Russian allies, see Yu. G. Saushkin, \u201cA History of Soviet Economic Geography\u201d (from N. N. Baranski et al., eds., Ekonomicheskaya Geografiya v SSSR), as translated in Soviet Geography: Review and Translation, Vol. 7, No. 8 (October, 1966), pp. 3\u2013104, especially pp. 78\u201388. 17 Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 16, p. 116. 18 Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 15, p. 172; see also p. 278 for a similar statement. The concept of the geographical environment as modified by man is widespread among Soviet geographers; it is closely related to the idea of the \u201cnoosphere,\u201d developed by \u201cthe founder of biogeochemistry,\u201d V. I. Vernadsky (1863-1945)\u2014a more concrete designation for the term \u201cnoosphere\u201d than used by Pierre Teilhard de Chardin. Anuchin pays occasional homage to Vernadsky, and so in considerable detail does Yu. G. Saushkin in \u201cMan and Nature,\u201dVestnik Moskovskogo Universiteta, Seriya Geografiya, 1969, No. 5, pp. 20\u201327; translated in Soviet Geography: Review and Translation, Vol. 11 (1970), pp. 707\u201314. 19 V. A. Anuchin, \u201cA Sad Tale about Geography,\u201dLiternaturnaya Gazeta, 18 February 1965, translated in Soviet Geography: Review and Translation, Vol. 6, No. 7 (September, 1965), pp. 27\u201331, claimed that \u201cThe environment \u2026 is a distinct part of external nature, a distinct form of matter that constitutes a material unity with society. The influence of the geographic environment is not only the influence of earth's nature, but also the influence of the human labor of all the \u2018societies\u2019 that have existed on earth and whose work has been embodied in nature.\u201d (p. 29) More characteristic of his writings is the passage in Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 15, p. 275, which speaks of the allegedly incorrect \u201cidea of the conditioning of all social phenomena by geographical factors,\u201d and in the same sentence implies to be correct the concept of \u201ccausal interconnections and interconditioning in the development of natural and social phenomena\u201d\u2014thus using \u201cgeographical\u201d and \u201cnatural\u201d essentially as synonyms. Social phenomena are often contrasted to \u201cgeographical\u201d phenomena elsewhere in the same book (e.g., pp. 94, 276, 279, and 285). 20 Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 15, p. 95, claims that geographical determinists fail to recognize qualitative differences between different phenomena on the earth; on p. 283 he says that \u201csociety as a whole always will be a qualitatively distinct part of the biosphere.\u201d He refers to Sechenov and Pavlov on p. 285. In a carefully constructed refutation of S. V. Kalesnik's dualism, on pp. 279\u201385, he tries to demonstrate how qualitatively different spheres of the material world can nevertheless interact causally with one another. 21 Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 15, p. 276. Anuchin declares his support for the basic tenor of the Stalinist dictum on p. 189 of his 1960 book (op. cit., footnote 16), and more explicitly on pp. 84\u201385 of his 1972 book (op. cit., footnote 15). The dictum appears on p. 26 of Dialectical and Historical Materialism (New York: International Publishers, 1940), an English translation of chapter four of the Short Course on the History of the Communist Party of the Soviet Union (Bolshevik), published in 1938 under the name of Joseph Stalin. 22 Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 15, pp. 92\u201393. 23 Georgi Plekhanov, The Development of the Monist View of History (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1956), \u201cPublisher's Foreword,\u201d p. 8. 24 Plekhanov, op. cit., footnote 23, pp. 157 and 161. 25 Anuchin cites Plekhanov's views in many passages, including Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 16, pp. 41\u201347 and 157\u201358, and Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 15, pp. 51\u201352, 269, and 292\u201393. Mechnikov's Civilization and the Great Historical Rivers, first published in French in 1889, is quoted on the changing significance of the environment in Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 15, p. 51; \u201csequent occupance,\u201d as expounded by Derwent Whittlesey and Preston James, amounts to another version of this argument. 26 Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 15, pp. 279 ff.; quotation from p. 279. See also footnote 20. 27 Quotation from Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 15, p. 19; even more detail on Montesquieu is given in Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 16, pp. 37\u201338. On the less pronounced non-environmental-determinist side of Montesquieu's thought, see Clarence J. Glacken, Traces on the Rhodian Shore (Berkeley and Los Angeles: University of California Press, 1967), pp. 576\u201381; and Karl M. Kriesel, \u201cMontesquieu: Possibilistic Geographer,\u201dAnnals, Association of American Geographers, Vol. 58 (1968), pp. 557 74. 28 General Soviet Marxist philosophy is discussed in such sources as George Lichtheim, Marxism (New York: Praeger, 1961); Alfred G. Meyer, Leninism (New York: Praeger, 1962); and Richard T. De-George, Patterns of Soviet Thought (Ann Arbor: University of Michigan Press, 1966). 29 Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 15, p. 47. Anuchin concedes that Huntington's determinism was tripartite, mixing environment, heredity, and culture, but dismisses his results as \u201cracist, Malthusian, and geopolitical pseudotheories.\u201d He so far misunderstands Taylor's environmental-determinist leanings that he claims the compendium Taylor edited, Geography in the Twentieth Century (1951), denied any interaction between environment and society. (He may have confused it with American Geography: Inventory and Prospect.) Huntington, Taylor, and Platt were discussed in such nearly identical words in the 1960 book that evidently Anuchin has learned nothing about them in the meantime; Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 16, pp. 80\u201381. Nevertheless, Anuchin has come a long way from the common situation only a couple of decades ago, when Soviet writers considered most contemporary Western geographers to be environmental determinists; we should congratulate our Russian counterparts on what they have learned about our views in the short time since, rather than berate them for what they still do not know, especially since our own attention to their work seems so slight by comparison. 30 Robert S. Platt, \u201cDeterminism in Geography,\u201dAnnals, Association of American Geographers, Vol. 38 (1948), pp. 126 32; William James is cited on pp. 129\u201330. 31 William W. Bunge, Teoreticheskaya Geografiya, originally published as Theoretical Geography (Lund: University of Lund Department of Geography, 1962). 32 Bunge's book is discussed in Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 15, pp. 109\u201311 and 120; quotations from pp. 109 and 111 (italics in original). Skepticism about mathematical geography is found on p. 215; Anuchin suspects it may be often used without any real need, just to be stylish. A review of Bunge's book by M. I. Al'brut, disagreeing with Yu. G. Saushkin's favorable impression, was translated in Soviet Geography: Review and Translation, Vol. 11 (1970), pp. 138\u201343. Bunge answered Al'brut in \u201cEthics and Logic in Geography,\u201d in Richard J. Chorley, ed., Directions in Geography (London: Methuen, 1973), pp. 317\u201331. 33 William W. Bunge, The First Years of the Detroit Geographical Expedition (Detroit: Society for Human Exploration, 1969); and William W. Bunge, Fitzgerald: Geography of a Revolution (Cambridge, Mass.: Schenkman, 1971; distributed by General Learning Press, Morristown, N.J.). Bunge calls for more attention to environmentalism\u201d in The Geography,\u201dProfessional Geographer, Vol. 25 (1973), pp. 331 37; see also Bunge's letter, Professional Geographer, Vol. 26 (1974), pp. 104\u201305, which comments on Anuchin's views. 34 Edward J. Taaffe, ed., Geography (Englewood Cliffs, N.J.: Prentice-Hall, 1970); The Science of Geography, Report of Ad Hoc Committee on Geography, chaired by Edward Ackerman (Washington, D.C.: National Academy of Sciences-National Research Council, 1965). Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 15, pp. 113\u201314; quotations from p. 114. Similar but less negative comments on The Science of Geography appear in Anuchin's On the Problems of Geography and the Tasks of Popularizing Geographical Knowledge (Moscow: Znanie RSFSR, 1968), as translated in Soviet Geography: Review and Translation, Vol. 11 (1970), pp. 82\u2013112; esp. pp. 107\u201308. Other American geographers mentioned briefly by Anuchin include Fred Lukermann, who is implied to adhere too closely to the allegedly unrealistic Kantian dichotomy between geography and history (p. 27; the Soviets pay very little attention to Sauer, but surely Anuchin would approve of his stand on this matter); Richard Hartshorne, who he believes has insufficiently differentiated between the \u201cidealist\u201d views of Ritter and the \u201cmaterialist\u201d geography of Humboldt (p. 33); Walter Isard, whose 1960 Methods of Regional Analysis (translated into Russian, 1966), along with a book by Peter Haggett, is said to take too much of an \u201cempirical-pragmatic approach\u201d to economic phenomena, and to lack adequate theory (p. 118); David Hooson, who is said to have overestimated the Marxist-Leninist quality of the dual geography of earlier Soviet years (p. 104), but who is praised for correctly evaluating the great influence of prerevolutionary Russian geographers on Soviet geography (p. 57); and Norton Ginsburg, who is given credit for correctly evaluating the extent to which the environment is taking on a social character, and thus must be viewed \u201cthrough the prism of human (p. is cited in Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 15, p. Anuchin for to the that influences really believes influences the which he says is essentially a rather than a social of see by translated in Soviet Geography: Review and Translation, Vol. 15 1974), p. is for his studies of climatic change and relation to history and in the are from Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 15, pp. 275, and (italics in original). Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 16, p. Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 16, p. 38 Russian of the approach to to have almost with similar in on Russian ecological including is in The of in the Soviet Union (Cambridge, Mass.: Press, and in in the Soviet Union (Cambridge, University Press, 1972). from Anuchin, op. cit., footnote 15, pp. 97 and of of Anuchin's of problems is on pp. of of not a to land can be found in op. cit., footnote p. ff.;